14811,282,Data analyst with skills in data visualization,Rainforest Alliance Consultants & Internships,Anywhere,"REQUEST FOR PROPOSALS

LandScale Data Visualization...

Rainforest Alliance is seeking a data analyst with skills in data visualization to assist LandScale assessment teams in communicating key landscape results graphically and informing the specifications of an online dashboard

SUMMARY INFORMATION
• Department: Advocacy and Themes
• Location(s): Remote
• Commitment: 15 – 25 days
• Desired start date and duration: January, February 2023
• Under direction of: Edita Chavez-Heredia

CONSULTANCY SUMMARY

About the Rainforest Alliance

Rainforest Alliance, a New York-based 501(c)3, and UTZ, an Amsterdam-based foundation,

merged in 2018. Together, the new Rainforest Alliance is now a $70 million per year organization

with offices in 17 countries (and growing) and over 5000 staff. The merged organization has two

main offices—one in Amsterdam and one in New York City.

The Rainforest Alliance is creating a more sustainable world by using social and market forces to

protect nature and improve the lives of farmers and forest communities.

We work towards making responsible business the new normal through our certification program,

tailored supply chain services, landscape and community work and advocacy.

Overview

LandScale (www.landscale.org) is an innovative new initiative, co-led by the Rainforest Alliance, Verra and Conservation International, which aims to drive improvements in sustainability at landscape scale. To do this, we have developed a system, comprising an online platform, an assessment framework and detailed guidance, and a validation mechanism, to assess and communicate trusted information about sustainability outcomes at the landscape-level. The LandScale system is available to any organization convening, implementing or financing work to improve sustainability at landscape scale. This includes governments, NGOs, donors, financial

institutions, companies producing or sourcing natural resource-based commodities, and the developers of nature-based solutions. The results of LandScale assessments are designed to help these organizations to do the following:
• Identify risks and opportunities: A flexible approach to understand landscape trends that can inform decision making and drive positive impact
• Convene stakeholders: A common language to align diverse stakeholders, identify shared goals, and catalyze cross-sectoral action
• Connect to incentives: Credible insights to unlock a variety of policy, market, and financial incentive mechanisms

The LandScale platform (www.platform.landscale.org) is a tool for building, reviewing, and sharing assessments of landscape sustainability performance. It provides assessment teams with a workspace to interact with the LandScale assessment framework, access to resources and LandScale support and a public-facing space to share their progress with investors and collaborators. This public-facing space will have interactive dashboards that ease the viewer into the complexities of the LandScale assessment results.

SCOPE OF WORK AND DELIVERABLES

Goal: Produce a series of data visualizations to convey LandScale assessment results and develop recommendations and data specifications for automated data visualizations that could be included in the LandScale public-facing results dashboards.

Objectives:

1. Work with LandScale customers to understand the scope and objectives of LandScale assessments and the shape and limitations of their data.

2. Identify and develop recommendations of tools, styles and formats for visualizing the results for each LandScale metric, individually and/or aggregated with other metric results.

3. Design a standard set of visualizations that LandScale users can select from in order to communicate assessment results;

4. Provide concrete recommendations on the best available software and programs that can be plugged- into the LandScale online platform to conduct the visualization of results depending on the type of data;

5. Develop required specifications of data inputs and outputs that would be needed for each recommended automated visualization on the results dashboard on the LandScale platform.

Activities

1. Meet with the LandScale team to understand the framework and platform functionalities to enter data. Discuss with assessment teams, in coordination with the LandScale team, their data needs and objectives. Review past assessment results to use them as the basis for the data visualization examples and proposals. Analyze collected insights and feedback to identify the requirements around data visualizations;

2. Advise assessment teams as to preferred format for data sharing in Excel or Google Sheets (and downloaded as a .csv file);

3. Research related data visualization initiatives/examples/efforts;

4. Provide recommendations on effective approaches to communicate LandScale results to decision makers, including landscape partnerships, government, investors, donors and companies sourcing from these landscapes

5. Join regular LandScale team meetings to present progress and obtain feedback;

6. Join meetings with LandScale’s online platform developer and UX consultant to discuss the required specifications on data inputs and outputs to inform and inform the development of an online platform;

Deliverables

1. Develop 40 data visualizations (5 visualizations per assessment, and a total of 8 assessments) based on completed key LandScale assessment results. The visualizations may represent:

a. Qualitative, quantitative, spatial, non-spatial, single data points (e.g., scatter plot, line chart, bar chart) or time series;

b. Results aggregated at different levels (geospatial, time, or LandScale-specific levels such as goals or indicators);

c. Results that have set targets (e.g., zero deforestation by 2030) (interim progress milestones (e.g., deforestation rate halved by 2025) in order to communicate the results in the context of those desired outcomes;

d. Results that do not have a target and/or milestone (i.e., present ideas to contextualize results in the absence of targets and milestones e.g., compared to national or regional trends);

2. Technical recommendations in relation to the potential integrations or functions that the LandScale online platform should consider in order to automatically create the chosen data visualizations:

a. Mockups of layouts for data inputs and outputs in the context of automated and standardized visualizations;

b. Recommendations on data models (including inputs required for each visualization) required for each visualization recommended.

c. Cost-conscious proposal on potential APIs with existing programs specialized in data visualizations;

d. Considerations on how to standardize, automate, and customize visualizations (e.g., interactive versus static visualizations).

e. Considerations on when these automations may fail, and how to handle those cases.

QUALIFICATIONS

1. Familiarity with R programming language, Power BI, Tableau, and/or Excel/Google Sheets, or any other conventional visualization program;

2. Experience accessing the codes provided in the GitHub repository for visualizations primarily uses R packages from the tidyverse (https://www.tidyverse.org/), and, specifically for plotting, ggplot2 (https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/).

GENERAL TERMS AND CONDITIONS FOR REQUEST FOR PROPOSAL
• Proposals and/or any additional information received after the submission time and date are subject to rejection by The Rainforest Alliance. Incomplete proposals may be rejected by RA at its discretion.
• The Rainforest Alliance reserves the right to request additional information at any time during the procurement process.
• RA is at liberty to make multiple or no selections as part of the proposal process. RA also reserves the right to cancel the procurement.
• All proposal costs shall be borne by the applicant and will not be reimbursed by RA.

SELECTION CRITERIA
• Cost
• Availability to conduct the assessment before the end of March 2023
• Demonstrated experience and understanding of complex data visualization and the requirements to automate and standardized them

PROPOSAL PROCESS AND FORMAT
• Proposals must be submitted to Edita Chavez by email (echavez@ra.org) by no later than 20th January
• Proposals should include:

o Overview of the candidate individual or team and their CV(s)

o Team portfolio of past work

o Budget

o Indicative workplan and timeline

o Initial thoughts on programs to develop data visualizations

Indication of individuals/organizations that would be consulted as part of this work if any

﻿Thank you for your interest in working with The Rainforest Alliance",2022-02-14,,data analyst,United States,"['tableau', 'power_bi', 'ggplot2', 'c', 'github', 'excel', 'r']"
