17868,3339,Medical Economist | Healthcare Economist,Upwork,Anywhere,"We are looking for an experienced Health Care and/or Medical Economist for a 3 to 6-month project. You will be working with the Company's executive team to plan and complete a scholarly study exploring the hyperescalation of perioperative personnel (anesthesiologists and CRNAs) compensation and its historical, current and prospective impact on the healthcare system given supply-demand kinetics... and the stable/declining professional service fee reimbursement trends.

This highly detailed study will examine the key drivers (clinician supply, clinician demand) that have led, and are leading, to the above compensation hyperescalation and examine how this hyperescalation is and will threaten the solvency of hospitals and the healthcare system, or portions thereof. Additionally, scenarios that impact supply, demand, and reimbursement will be studied and used to project forward how they influence system solvency issuess,

In non-technical parlance, hospitals are paid for surgical services, a per-procedure fee based on the diagnostic-related group [DRG] into which the procedure falls, This fee can be adjusted based on a variety of factors (type of hospital, presence of physician residents, the presence/absence of major comorbidities, etc.). From this payment, all expenses incidental to the DRG episode of surgical care are paid. This includes nursing personnel, facility, ancillary staff, administrative personnel, medications, supplies, equipment, ICU stays, hosptial stays, etc. It does not inclide the cost for professional service fees for physicans and specialized nurses. There is an incremental margin on a per-procedure basis ascribed to a given patient, and this rolls up to a total contributory margin ascribed to the operating rooms or perioperative service in total. Parenthetically, from a hospital-wide margin perspective, the operating rooms or the perioperative service is an anchor service line , often contributing the most, or nearly the most of any otehr service line.

Most hopsitals' anesthesia service is cash flow negative and requires substantial financal support from the hospital which serves to erode each level of the margin-chain (patient, service line, hospital). Hyperescalation of compensation without increases in DRG or professional fee reimbursments, accentuates this margin depression.

Our thesis is that hyperescation of anesthesiologist and CRNA (likely surgeons too!) compensation along with stable/declning reimbursement can have an impact threatening hospitals and the healthcare system. Moroever, we wish to describe and demonstrate (using data and analytics) how different solutions centered around or adressing the the traid of drivers(Supply,demand, reimbursement) can change the trajectory. For instance, solutions might include more residency programs/CRNA schools, greater operating room efficiency permitting a smaller coverage footprint, and government and commercail payors increasing their rates.

The ideal candidate will know where and how to obtain data on the driver-triad, anf then how to anlyze it to prove or disprove our hypothesis",2022-02-26,Contractor,data analyst,United States,[]
17869,3340,Tableau -Data Analytics and Calculation,Upwork,Anywhere,"Hello,

I'm current running into a critical issue, and I hope the community can help me with this...

Attached is a sample data source and workbook of the activity I'm working on. The data source is the number of receipts received from vendor ""HL INDUSTRIES"". Also, in the data source contained the nonconformance number of the reject in each receipt. The receipt number is RISN and its unique with a Date Inspection, and the nonconformance number is NC Number Num with a NC create date.

I pivot the Date Inspection and NC Date Created to get a count without conflicting the nonconformance and receipt number later when filtering. So, the date will be now ""Date"" from the pivot of the two dates and counting the two dates using Data Type.

My calculation for Nonconformance:

COUNTD(IF [Date Type]='Nc Date Creat' THEN [Nc Number Num] END)

My calculation for Receipt:

FIXED MONTH([Date]): COUNTD(IF [Date Type]='Date Insp' THEN [Risn] END)

I want to have count nonconformance to be independent from receipt. However, without filtering, I'm getting the right number, but when I'm filtering NC Type and NC Factory Code to RI and Supplier, respectively, the filter removed other data I want to see in receipt. See Images attached and files attached.

I want to be able to see all the receipt count, but the NC to be filter to ""RI"" and ""Supplier"" for NC Type and NC Factory Code. Like the first picture with all receipt counts, but only the count of nonconformance from the second picture",2022-01-28,Contractor,data analyst,United States,[]
