21136,585,Help Center Junior Data Analyst,California Department of Managed Health Care,Anywhere,"Job Description and Duties

The Help Center assists consumers with health care issues and through its regulatory authority ensures that managed health care enrollees receive the medical care and services to which they are entitled...

Work in a collaborative and supportive team! Under the general direction of the Data Analytics Unit (DAU) Research Data Supervisor I (RDSup I), the Research Data Analyst I (RDA I) will serve as a Help Center Junior Data Analyst performing basic research, data gathering, scheduled report maintenance, and statistical work for DMHC management and other stakeholder groups. The RDA I will run routine and timely reports, respond to ad-hoc requests for data and reports, and assist Help Center data analysts, senior data analysts, and management in data entry, basic data analyses, and workflow.

This is primarily a telework position and may require in-person attendance when requested.

A Training and Development (T&D) assignment can be considered for this position.

This position is eligible for full-time telework under Government Code 14200 for eligible applicants residing in California. Remote-centered teleworker employees may be required to report in person to headquarters and/or offsite events or locations, as determined by management, and will be required to utilize a shared workspace or “hotel” when working in the office.

Working Conditions

The DMHC utilizes a hybrid telework model to provide all employees with an avenue to telework while ensuring business and operational needs are met.

Remote-Centered employees are expected to maintain a safe and distraction free work environment at the approved alternate work location. Remote-Centered employees agree to adhere to the state telework policy, the DMHC’s telework policy, and conditions cited in the Telework Agreement (STD 200).

Office-Centered employees are expected to maintain a dedicated workstation at a DMHC official worksite. Office-Centered employees are expected to work in a climate-controlled office or cubicle under artificial lighting.

